    "European batteries are cold-cranking rated to EN 50342, not SAE CCA. The two tests measure different things, so the DIN88's 800A EN and the DIN100's 870A EN are not directly comparable to a JIS or BCI CCA figure — always compare within the same standard.",
    "Reserve Capacity follows the SAE J537 definition: minutes a fully charged battery sustains a 25-amp load at 27°C (80°F) before reaching 10.5V. The DIN88's 150 minutes and the DIN100's 170 minutes are the buffer for lighting and telematics when the engine is off or the alternator fails.",
    "Capacity is measured at the 20-hour rate (C20). The DIN88's 88Ah and the DIN100's 100Ah describe total energy storage over a slow 20-hour discharge — a different measure from the short burst the EN rating describes.",
